WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.336
("Rule Britannia")

00:00:02.336 --> 00:00:03.521
- [Narrator] In the United Kingdom,

00:00:03.521 --> 00:00:06.705
you may eat ducks, chickens, geese,

00:00:06.705 --> 00:00:09.327
and, nah, not these.
(swan squawks)

00:00:09.327 --> 00:00:12.206
They all belong to the queen, well…

00:00:12.206 --> 00:00:16.386
- All swans in the United
Kingdom can belong to the queen,

00:00:16.386 --> 00:00:20.418
but she mainly exercises this
right on the River Thames.

00:00:20.418 --> 00:00:22.918
- [Narrator] Of course, sorry.

00:00:26.798 --> 00:00:30.344
- My name's David Barber,
and I look after the swans

00:00:30.344 --> 00:00:35.180
wherever the queen exercises
her royal prerogative right.

00:00:35.180 --> 00:00:36.789
It's not a full-time job,

00:00:36.789 --> 00:00:40.131
although sometimes it
feels like a full-time job.

00:00:40.131 --> 00:00:42.119
- [Narrator] Every year
Mr. Barber runs a census

00:00:42.119 --> 00:00:45.879
of the swans for Her Majesty,
a bit like an annual checkup.

00:00:45.879 --> 00:00:49.130
This is called swan upping, and
he does it with a whole crew

00:00:49.130 --> 00:00:52.608
like this guy, this guy, and this guy.

00:00:52.608 --> 00:00:56.261
- Swan upping has taken
place since the 12th century

00:00:56.261 --> 00:00:59.020
where swans in those days were actually

00:00:59.020 --> 00:01:01.739
a very, very important food.

00:01:01.739 --> 00:01:05.753
Of course, today swans
are a protected species

00:01:05.753 --> 00:01:07.420
and no longer eaten.

00:01:09.007 --> 00:01:11.980
We travel in six traditional rowing skiffs

00:01:11.980 --> 00:01:15.478
from Sunbury-On-Thames all
the way up to Abingdon,

00:01:15.478 --> 00:01:18.474
and it's about a 79-mile journey.

00:01:18.474 --> 00:01:20.111
- What do we say when we see a swan?

00:01:20.111 --> 00:01:21.403
- 'Ohh,
- 'Ohh,

00:01:21.403 --> 00:01:22.986
- 'old up, 'old up.’

00:01:24.405 --> 00:01:26.565
- [David] Each family
of swans we come across,

00:01:26.565 --> 00:01:29.168
we will circle the skiffs around them.

00:01:29.168 --> 00:01:31.364
We will tie the wings and their legs.

00:01:31.364 --> 00:01:33.194
We will take them ashore.

00:01:33.194 --> 00:01:35.262
We will weigh them, measure them,

00:01:35.262 --> 00:01:37.739
and check them for any injuries.

00:01:37.739 --> 00:01:40.091
After we've done that, we
will put a ring on them,

00:01:40.091 --> 00:01:43.417
and we will release them
back into the river.

00:01:43.417 --> 00:01:45.345
- [Narrator] So if these
are the queen's swans,

00:01:45.345 --> 00:01:47.529
where is Her Majesty for all of this?

00:01:47.529 --> 00:01:50.079
- [David] She did come swan
upping a few year's ago,

00:01:50.079 --> 00:01:52.007
and hopefully, she enjoyed it.

00:01:52.007 --> 00:01:53.687
She seemed to enjoy it,

00:01:53.687 --> 00:01:57.362
but I can't answer for the queen anyway.

00:01:57.362 --> 00:02:00.105
I love swans, a royal bird.

00:02:00.105 --> 00:02:03.459
People here in the United
Kingdom, they love them.

00:02:03.459 --> 00:02:05.449
And you know, I want to make sure

00:02:05.449 --> 00:02:08.226
that they survive for the future.

00:02:08.226 --> 00:02:10.377
- [Narrator] All right,
gents, let's toast.

00:02:10.377 --> 00:02:11.642
- [Dave] To the queen.

00:02:11.642 --> 00:02:13.392
- [All] To the queen.

00:02:18.781 --> 00:02:21.614
(electronic note)

